</head><body><header><div><h1>My Writings & Reflections — Living with COPD</h1><small>By Joe Ling © 2026</small></div></header><main class="book-wrap"><div class="book" id="book"></div></main><div class="nav"><button id="prev" onclick="turn(-1)">❮ Back</button><button id="home-btn" onclick="go(0)">⌂ Home</button><button id="index-btn" onclick="showIndex()">☷ Index</button><button id="next" onclick="turn(1)">Next ❯</button><span id="position"></span></div><div class="modalback" id="overlay" onclick="if(event.target===this)closeModal()"><div class="modal" id="modal"></div></div><script type="application/json" id="book-snapshot"></script><script>
const originalCover='data:image/webp;base64,';
const KEY='joe-copd-book-v1';let data;try{data=JSON.parse((null))}catch(e){}if(!data||!Array.isArray(data.articles))data={articles:[],notices:'',intro:'Over the years I’ve written quite a lot about living with COPD, not so much about the medical side but about the everyday challenges, experiences and emotions that come with it.'};if(!data.articles.some(a=>a.title==="Exercising is a good way to meet people.")){data.articles.unshift({"title": "Exercising is a good way to meet people.", "date": "2026-11-08", "text": "Today whilst exercising,  I met an ambulance driver, a paramedic, three nurses, a doctor, a priest . .  . and almost met Jesus as well.\n\nNext time I’ll try yoga. Probably safer.for my health. There you only meet incense, soft chanting and highly flexible denial. 😆\n\nHappy weekend everyone 💜", "image": "data:image/jpeg;base64,", "video": ""});/* No browser persistence. */}let current=0;
if(!data.articles.some(a=>a.title==='Understanding the Invisible Struggle')){data.articles.push({"title": "Understanding the Invisible Struggle", "date": "2026-11-08", "text": "Today, I'm going to discuss chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, commonly known as COPD. This presentation is titled Understanding the Invisible Struggle, because COPD, while severe, is often not visible to others.\n\nOur goal is to help you understand the challenges faced by those living with this condition. I want to start with a powerful statement. I may not look sick, but lungs don't show.\n\nIf I can't breathe, nothing else matters. This captures the essence of living with COPD. It's a serious, progressive illness that profoundly impacts daily life, even when it's not immediately apparent to others.\n\nPeople with COPD often struggle silently. Their challenge is invisible to the world around them. Let's break down what COPD actually is.\n\nFirst, COPD is not a single disease, but a group of lung diseases that make it hard to breathe. This means the impact can vary from person to person. It includes illnesses like emphysema and chronic bronchitis.\n\nThese conditions damage the lungs over time, making breathing increasingly difficult. The main symptom of COPD is shortness of breath, especially during activity. This means that tasks you and I might take for granted, like climbing stairs or carrying groceries, can become extremely challenging for someone with COPD.\n\nUnderstanding these basics helps us grasp the daily struggles faced by those with emphysema or chronic bronchitis. Living with COPD is challenging in ways that might not be immediately obvious. It affects every aspect of a person's life, their physical abilities, social interactions, emotional well-being, and family dynamics.\n\nIn the following slides, we'll explore these challenges in detail to give you a comprehensive understanding of what it means to live with COPD. One of the major challenges of living with COPD is avoiding triggers. Certain things can irritate the lungs and make symptoms worse.\n\nThese are called triggers. Common triggers include smoke, dust, pollen, and strong chemicals. This means that everyday environments can pose risks.\n\nFor someone with COPD, a walk in a park on a high pollen day or entering a freshly cleaned room with strong chemical smells could trigger severe breathing difficulties. This need for constant vigilance adds an extra layer of stress to daily life. It can limit where a person can go and what activities they can participate in.\n\nYour understanding and support in creating and maintaining trigger-free environments can make a significant difference in the quality of life for someone with COPD. COPD presents significant physical challenges. It can make it difficult to be as active as one used to be.\n\nThis loss of physical capability often leads to feelings of frustration and helplessness. People with COPD may find it hard to climb stairs. What was once a simple task can become an exhausting ordeal.\n\nWalking long distances can be challenging. A stroll in the park or a trip to the grocery store might require careful planning and rest breaks. Keeping up with others becomes difficult.\n\nThis can affect work life, social activities, and family interactions. These physical limitations can lead to a loss of independence and significantly impact a person's quality of life. COPD can have profound social impacts.\n\nIt can make it hard to socialise. The fear of having breathing difficulties in public can lead to social withdrawal. People with COPD may feel embarrassed to cough in public.\n\nThis can make them hesitant to participate in social gatherings or public events. They might feel self-conscious about using oxygen or other medical equipment. This can lead to avoiding situations where they might need to use these devices in front of others.\n\nThese factors combined can lead to isolation and loneliness, which can further impact their overall health and well-being. Your encouragement and understanding can help a person with COPD maintain social connections and feel more comfortable in social situations. COPD takes significant toll on emotional well-being.\n\nIt can lead to frustration. The inability to do things they once could easily accomplish can be incredibly frustrating. Anger is a common emotion.\n\nPeople might feel angry at the unfairness of the disease or the limitations it imposes on their life. Fear is often present. There's fear about the progression of the disease, fear of the next flare-up, and fear about the future.\n\nDepression and anxiety are common among people with COPD due to these ongoing challenges. Recognising and addressing these emotional impacts is crucial for overall health and quality of life for someone with COPD. COPD doesn't just affect the individual.\n\nIt impacts the entire family. Loved ones may need to help with daily activities. This can change family dynamics and responsibilities.\n\nFamily members often need to provide emotional support, which can be emotionally taxing for them as well. The role of caregiver may fall to family members, which is a significant responsibility. Family plans and activities might need to be adjusted to accommodate the needs of the person with COPD.\n\nIt's important for family members to also seek support and take care of their own well-being while supporting their loved one with COPD. As we conclude, I want to emphasise two crucial points. I may not look sick.\n\nSick lungs don't show. This reminds us that COPD is often an invisible illness. The person in front of you may appear fine, but they're fighting a battle with every breath.\n\nIf I can't breathe, nothing else matters. This powerful statement encapsulates the challenge of living with COPD. The struggle is internal, not always visible to the outside world.\n\nBy understanding and acknowledging these struggles, you can provide the support and compassion needed. Your understanding and support can make a world of difference in the life of someone with COPD. Thank you for taking the time to learn about COPD and its impact on individuals and families.\n\nNow, I'd like to invite you to take action and make a difference in the lives of those affected by COPD right here in Thailand. Join us on Facebook by searching for COPD Support Thailand. Although still small, this group is more than just a page.\n\nIt's a community of patients, caregivers, family members, and healthcare professionals coming together to support one another. Join me now and be part of a community that truly understands and cares.", "image": "", "video": "builtin-invisible-struggle"});}
if(!data.articles.some(a=>a.title==='A Life in Verse — Last Breath of a Traveller — One Breath At A Time')){let position=data.articles.findIndex(a=>a.title==='Understanding the Invisible Struggle');data.articles.splice(position<0?data.articles.length:position,0,{"title": "A Life in Verse — Last Breath of a Traveller — One Breath At A Time", "date": "2026-05-30", "text": "A Life in Verse\nLast Breath of a Traveller\nOne Breath At A Time\nBy Joe Ling - 30 May 2026\n\nBorn in a time of dreams and strife,\nI danced on the edge, I lived a wild life.\nStowed away on ships, crossed deserts so vast,\nIn jungles and films, my shadow was cast.\nWith tigers I played, with danger I'd flirt,\nNomadic by nature, seeking no cert.\nThailand became my sanctuary sweet,\nIn simple joys, my soul found its beat.\n\nBut fate, with its cruel and unpredictable hand,\nStruck me down where I could barely stand.\nWhat started as a whisper in my throat,\nSoon turned to a storm, a dark, sinking boat.\nCancer and COPD, a double-edged sword,\nEach breath now a battle, each step a reward.\nThe man who once conquered deserts and seas,\nNow struggles to breathe, brought down to his knees.\n\nI really hate the illness, it poisons my mind,\nTwisting my spirit, once gentle and kind.\nAt times I don't even recognize me,\nAnger flares where calm used to be.\nI've always been patient, my voice was never raised,\nBut now I lash out, my temper ablaze.\nAt my foster son for the smallest mistake,\nRegret floods in, my heart starts to ache.\n\nI long to repair the wounds I've inflicted,\nTo ease the pain we both have endured.\n\nMy backpack replaced by oxygen's weight,\nAdventure a memory, my world now so great.\nShowering, walking—tasks so small,\nNow planned like adventures, each one a haul.\nThe chats with strangers, now few and far,\nMy social life dimmed, just a flickering star.\nIn a foreign land, the hurdles are steep,\nLanguage and culture, a labyrinth deep.\n\nMoney's a worry, a shadow that looms,\nWith rent and meds, a constant gloom.\nBetrayed by a partner, left in the lurch,\nJustice, a dream in a foreigner's search.\nMy foster son Timmy, my anchor, my light,\nNow drifts away, lost in his own fight.\nI understand, yet the pain is so real,\nLoneliness, a wound that time cannot heal.\n\nDepression, a shadow that follows my days,\nA mirror that shows me my life's lost ways.\nFatigue and despair, my constant refrain,\nYet in my heart, a flicker remains.\nFor in every fall, I've found my ground,\nAnd this time too, I won't be bound.\nMy journey continues, though the path is unclear,\nWith a heart full of hope, I'll conquer my fear.\n\nTo those who walk this path of despair,\nKnow that your struggle, I deeply share.\nDon't give up, though the night is long,\nIn the darkest hour, we can still be strong.\nTo the world around us, your kindness, your care,\nIs a beacon of hope in the darkest of air.\nOne breath at a time, one step each day,\nTogether we'll find our way.\n\nI hold on to hope, to the joy that remains,\nEven if it's different, even if it pains.\nFor in this foreign land, I've found a home,\nIn each friendly face, I'm not alone.\nSo if my story touches your heart,\nLend a hand, play your part.\nFor every small act of love you show,\nIs a lifeline, a light, in a world full of woe.", "image": "", "video": "builtin-life-in-verse"});}
if(!data.articles.some(a=>a.title==="When Fear, Frustration and Love Collide")){data.articles.push({"title": "When Fear, Frustration and Love Collide", "date": "2026-11-08", "text": "👉 𝗧𝗥𝗬 𝗜𝗧 - 𝗜𝗧𝗦 𝗙𝗥𝗘𝗘 👈\n𝗪𝗛𝗘𝗡 𝗙𝗘𝗔𝗥, 𝗙𝗥𝗨𝗦𝗧𝗥𝗔𝗧𝗜𝗢𝗡 𝗔𝗡𝗗 𝗟𝗢𝗩𝗘 𝗖𝗢𝗟𝗟𝗜𝗗𝗘 ❤️\n\nLast night, a conversation really upset me. Noelle's story about her 14-year-old daughter and William's reply about a 15-year-old brought back painful memories of my own children and the heartache my illness and struggles have caused them.\n\nWe feel misunderstood but so do our children. Understanding has to work both ways. Sometimes, in our own pain, we forget how much they're hurting too.\n\nI was so upset by those conversations that I could hardly sleep, so at 03:30 this morning, I found myself sitting under my favourite tree, still thinking about those families and the heartache my own children had experienced.\n\nThen I remembered an unfinished project I'd started in 2022 to help my foster son, Timmy, who was just 14 at the time. So I started working and after several cappuccinos, many hours of work, and a few arguments with my Android phone, I finally brought it to life! 🤣\n\n𝗜𝗡𝗧𝗥𝗢𝗗𝗨𝗖𝗜𝗡𝗚 𝗠𝗬 𝗙𝗥𝗘𝗘 𝗖𝗢𝗣𝗗 𝗘𝗠𝗘𝗥𝗚𝗘𝗡𝗖𝗬 𝗦𝗨𝗣𝗣𝗢𝗥𝗧 𝗔𝗣𝗣 ❤️\n𝘝𝘦𝘳𝘴𝘪𝘰𝘯 5.0 — 𝘙𝘦𝘭𝘦𝘢𝘴𝘦 𝘊𝘢𝘯𝘥𝘪𝘥𝘢𝘵𝘦\n\nOriginally, I designed this specifically for teenagers who find themselves caring for a parent or another adult living with severe COPD, often with very little guidance or support.\n\nAt an age when they're already struggling with puberty, changing hormones, emotions and finding their own place in the world, they may also be dealing with the fear of losing someone they love, responsibilities they never asked for and situations even adults find overwhelming.\n\nWe expect them to understand our illness but how often do we stop to understand what they're going through themselves?\n\nIt started with a simple video explaining how COPD affects everyday life, followed by an emergency poster for the fridge. One thing led to another and eventually it grew into this app, providing practical information, emergency contacts, a call script and a practice mode to help young carers and their families feel more informed, supported and prepared.\n\nSo during a sleepless night, I created a small app to make their life a bit easier in an emergency when everyone is stressed out already\n\nSometimes helping our children understand our struggles with COPD starts with understanding theirs. ❤️\n\n👉 𝗧𝗥𝗬 𝗜𝗧 - 𝗜𝗧𝗦 𝗙𝗥𝗘𝗘 🙏\n𝘑𝘶𝘴𝘵 𝘨𝘪𝘷𝘦 𝘮𝘦 𝘩𝘰𝘯𝘦𝘴𝘵 𝘧𝘦𝘦𝘥𝘣𝘢𝘤𝘬\n𝘢𝘯𝘥 𝘪𝘧 𝘺𝘰𝘶 𝘭𝘪𝘬𝘦 𝘪𝘵, 𝘐 𝘯𝘦𝘷𝘦𝘳 𝘴𝘢𝘺 𝘯𝘰 𝘵𝘰 𝘢 𝘤𝘰𝘧𝘧𝘦𝘦\n\nhttps://joelingmonkey.github.io/copd-emergency-app-/\n\n⚠️ 𝗜𝗠𝗣𝗢𝗥𝗧𝗔𝗡𝗧:\n𝘐'𝘮 𝘯𝘰𝘵 𝘢 𝘮𝘦𝘥𝘪𝘤𝘢𝘭 𝘦𝘹𝘱𝘦𝘳𝘵 𝘢𝘯𝘥 𝘵𝘩𝘪𝘴 𝘪𝘴𝘯'𝘵 𝘮𝘦𝘥𝘪𝘤𝘢𝘭 𝘢𝘥𝘷𝘪𝘤𝘦. 𝘛𝘩𝘦 𝘢𝘱𝘱 𝘪𝘴 𝘴𝘵𝘪𝘭𝘭 𝘣𝘦𝘪𝘯𝘨 𝘵𝘦𝘴𝘵𝘦𝘥 𝘢𝘯𝘥 𝘪𝘵𝘴 𝘮𝘦𝘥𝘪𝘤𝘢𝘭 𝘪𝘯𝘴𝘵𝘳𝘶𝘤𝘵𝘪𝘰𝘯𝘴 𝘢𝘳𝘦 𝘶𝘯𝘥𝘦𝘳𝘨𝘰𝘪𝘯𝘨 𝘳𝘦𝘷𝘪𝘦𝘸 𝘣𝘺 𝘢 𝘥𝘰𝘤𝘵𝘰𝘳. 𝘗𝘭𝘦𝘢𝘴𝘦 𝘶𝘴𝘦 𝘧𝘪𝘤𝘵𝘪𝘰𝘯𝘢𝘭 𝘪𝘯𝘧𝘰𝘳𝘮𝘢𝘵𝘪𝘰𝘯 𝘧𝘰𝘳 𝘵𝘦𝘴𝘵𝘪𝘯𝘨 𝘢𝘯𝘥 𝘥𝘰𝘯'𝘵 𝘳𝘦𝘭𝘺 𝘰𝘯 𝘪𝘵 𝘥𝘶𝘳𝘪𝘯𝘨 𝘢 𝘳𝘦𝘢𝘭 𝘦𝘮𝘦𝘳𝘨𝘦𝘯𝘤𝘺. 𝘐𝘵'𝘴 𝘯𝘰𝘵 𝘢 𝘴𝘦𝘤𝘶𝘳𝘦 𝘮𝘦𝘥𝘪𝘤𝘢𝘭-𝘳𝘦𝘤𝘰𝘳𝘥 𝘴𝘺𝘴𝘵𝘦𝘮.\n\n𝗡𝗢𝗪 𝗜 𝗡𝗘𝗘𝗗 𝗬𝗢𝗨𝗥 𝗛𝗘𝗟𝗣 ❤️\nPlease give it a try and tell me what's useful, confusing or missing. Don't worry about hurting my feelings. I've survived chemotherapy, radiotherapy, severe emphysema and a few encounters with the Grim Reaper. I can survive constructive criticism! 🤣\n\nIf this little project eventually helps even one family understand each other better or feel more prepared, those hours under my tree were worth it and if you find it useful, I'm always happy to accept a cappuccino! ☕🤣\n\n𝙍𝙚𝙖𝙡 𝙋𝙚𝙤𝙥𝙡𝙚 • 𝙍𝙚𝙖𝙡 𝙀𝙭𝙥𝙚𝙧𝙞𝙚𝙣𝙘𝙚𝙨 • 𝙍𝙚𝙖𝙡 𝙎𝙪𝙥𝙥𝙤𝙧𝙩 ❤️\n\nJoe Ling\n\n𝘗.𝘚. 𝘍𝘰𝘭𝘭𝘰𝘸𝘪𝘯𝘨 𝘮𝘦 𝘳𝘦𝘮𝘢𝘪𝘯𝘴 𝘢 𝘲𝘶𝘦𝘴𝘵𝘪𝘰𝘯𝘢𝘣𝘭𝘦 𝘥𝘦𝘤𝘪𝘴𝘪𝘰𝘯. 𝘔𝘰𝘴𝘵 𝘰𝘧 𝘵𝘩𝘦 𝘵𝘪𝘮𝘦 𝘐'𝘮 𝘭𝘰𝘴𝘵 𝘮𝘺𝘴𝘦𝘭𝘧, 𝘥𝘳𝘪𝘧𝘵𝘪𝘯𝘨 𝘪𝘯 𝘶𝘯𝘬𝘯𝘰𝘸𝘯 𝘥𝘪𝘳𝘦𝘤𝘵𝘪𝘰𝘯𝘴. 𝘠𝘰𝘶'𝘳𝘦 𝘸𝘦𝘭𝘤𝘰𝘮𝘦 𝘢𝘭𝘰𝘯𝘨 𝘧𝘰𝘳 𝘵𝘩𝘦 𝘳𝘪𝘥𝘦! 🤣🧡", "image": "data:image/png;base64,", "video": ""});}
const topics=['How anger, frustration and stress can affect us.','Living with illness without allowing it to define who we are.','Hope, acceptance and learning to live with what we cannot change.','Illness, hope, religion, faith and the search for miracles.','Fear, anxiety and worrying about tomorrow.','Loneliness, solitude and learning to enjoy our own company.','Losing our independence and learning to accept help.','The emotional impact of COPD on family, friends and relationships.','The difference between giving up and accepting reality.','How our thoughts and attitudes influence our daily lives.','Why medical labels, statistics and numbers don’t define how we feel.','Finding happiness in the smallest everyday pleasures.','The importance of humour, even when life gets pretty shitty. 😜','Dealing with setbacks without losing hope.','Gratitude, compassion and supporting each other.','Learning to live for today instead of constantly fearing tomorrow.','Finding purpose and meaning in life despite our limitations.'];
function save(){/* In-memory edits only; use Export Complete Book to persist. */}function esc(s){return String(s??'').replace(/[&<>"']/g,c=>({'&':'&amp;','<':'&lt;','>':'&gt;','"':'&quot;',"'":'&#39;'}[c]))}function page(x){return '<section class="page">'+x+'</section>'}function spread(a,b){return '<div class="spread">'+page(a)+page(b)+'</div>'}const BUILTIN_VIDEO="data:video/mp4;base64,